A||||2||mshill||Pics of 86SE "Swiss Cheese"...||||10||
Z||000000||mshill||03-20-2000||03:03 AM||||Ok, since I wasn't able to go to Modesto, CA this last weekend, I was able to get the old "Cheese" back on the road.<P>Here's some shots of the 86SE V6 that all stock except for a cam, ground exhaust and radio.  Engine now has 20 miles on fresh rebuild and the new clutch rocks.<P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fier21_1.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fier22.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fier24.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fier25.jpg"> <P> <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/Fier27.jpg"> <P>I found the red cap and red wires, but couldn't get my hands on a red coil (at the moment).  Notice the red/orange air filter housing.  You cannot see them but both the alternator and A/C brackets are painted as well.<P><BR>||||reg||10
Z||000001||ChadMan||03-20-2000||08:35 AM||||Looks great.  But I'm curious - why the "Swiss Cheese" nickname?  Your Fiero obviously isn't full of holes...||||reg||5
Z||000002||mshill||03-20-2000||01:31 PM||||ChadMan,  the "Swiss Cheese" name came from when I first bought it.  I paid $750 for it cause the kid couldn't get it smogged here in California because it would overheat on the dyno.  When I looked at it the block was nearly bone dry and we filled it up before I took it home.  Once in my driveway it had an obvious leak.  Well, that was just the beginning.  I would replace one hose and another would start leaking, and so on.  Well, I got it to where I thought it would hold water and took it to get smogged.  While on the dyno the radiator blew a hole.  After that was replaced, the heater core went.<P>Needless to say that every coolant and heater hose in the entire car has been replaced.  I guess now it could just be called the "Cheese".<BR>||||reg||1
